<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Area of a Right Triangle Using Its Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]].')} /> Apply <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The result names the total flat area enclosed by the three sides of the triangle: six square units. This is exactly what the Right Triangle Area From Semiperimeter Calculator returns for these two inputs.</p>
  <p>The inradius is the radius of the circle tucked inside the triangle, touching all three sides. The semiperimeter is half the length of the boundary: the total of the three sides divided by two. Two measurements are enough because the inradius serves as a shared perpendicular height for three smaller triangles built from the incenter, while the semiperimeter gathers their bases into a single factor.</p>
  <p>The reasoning works for every triangle, because each one owns an incircle. The right triangle adds a bonus: its inradius follows from the three sides through a short formula, so r and s are often already known. This method shines in problems where the incircle or the semiperimeter is given, since no leg or angle needs measuring.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Right triangle geometry -->
<section id="sec-right-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Before the incircle enters the picture, the triangle itself must be labelled without error. The symbols below stay fixed through the whole guide, and the calculator uses the same names.</p>

  <h3>Definition of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a three-sided polygon containing exactly one interior angle of 90 degrees. It has three vertices, three sides, two acute angles, and one right angle usually marked with a small square. The two sides forming the right angle are the legs; the side opposite is the hypotenuse. Since <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />: the two acute angles are complementary, so knowing one determines the other.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Two Legs</h3>
  <p>Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />; leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> sits opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]].')} /> The two legs meet at the right-angle vertex and are perpendicular to each other. They contribute to the area directly, because each leg can serve as the base while the other serves as the perpendicular height. For this page they also feed the inradius, since the shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> is built from both legs and the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the 90-degree angle, always the longest side of the triangle. It never forms the right angle; it stretches across from it. In the legs area formula it plays no role, but here it matters: the hypotenuse enters both the semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> and the inradius shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]].')} /> If both legs are known, the Pythagorean theorem recovers it through <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>What Makes the Triangle Different From Other Triangles?</h3>
  <p>The 90-degree corner is the whole story. It makes the legs perpendicular without any construction, which simplifies the standard area formula, and it makes the incircle tangent segments near the right angle equal to the inradius itself, which simplifies the inradius formula. Other triangles still satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} />, but finding their inradius usually requires the area first. The right triangle is the one case where the inradius falls out of the side lengths directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Incircle -->
<section id="sec-incircle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Incircle of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The inradius only makes sense once the incircle is pictured clearly. This section builds the idea from the circle itself down to the tangent points where the radius meets each side.</p>

  <h3>Definition of an Incircle</h3>
  <p>An incircle is a circle positioned inside a triangle so that it touches all three sides. It sits in the interior, tangent to each side at exactly one point, and it cannot cross a side because tangency pins it in place. Every nondegenerate triangle owns exactly one incircle: shrink a circle inside the corner regions and there is a single largest size that kisses all three sides. The three points of tangency, one per side, are the places where the inradius will later be measured.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Incenter?</h3>
  <p>The incenter, labelled <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} />, is the point where the three internal angle bisectors meet. Each bisector splits one corner angle into two equal parts, and any point on a bisector sits at equal perpendicular distance from the two sides forming that angle. The intersection of all three bisectors therefore sits at equal distance from all three sides, which is exactly why it can serve as the center of a circle touching each side. The incenter lies inside every valid triangle, never on an edge or outside.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Inradius?</h3>
  <p>The inradius is the perpendicular distance from the incenter to any side of the triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\text{distance from the incenter to a side}}]]]')} />. Because the incenter is equidistant from all three sides, the value is the same no matter which side is chosen. That single number is the radius of the incircle, the length of each of the three short segments drawn from the center to the tangent points, and the shared height that makes the area proof in a later section work.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Tangent Relationship</h3>
  <p>A radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the side it touches. Applied to the three contact points, this gives <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp c]].')} /> Each statement describes a different corner of the diagram: the radius ending on leg a stands at right angles to leg a, and likewise for leg b and the hypotenuse. This perpendicularity is not decorative; it is the property that lets each radius act as the height of one of the three smaller triangles in the area proof.</p>

  <h3>Difference Between Incircle and Circumcircle</h3>
  <p>The incircle lives inside the triangle and touches all three sides; the circumcircle passes through all three vertices and encloses the triangle. Their radii are different measurements and must never be swapped. For a right triangle the circumradius has its own simple rule, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{R=\\frac{c}{2}}]]]')} />, because the hypotenuse is a diameter of the circumcircle: the midpoint of the hypotenuse is the circumcenter. The inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the smaller of the two and belongs to the circle inside.</p>

