    <img src={imgSrc('incircle-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with incircle: incenter I, three tangent points, perpendicular radii of length r" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC with its incircle: three perpendicular radii, each of length r.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-semiperimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Semiperimeter of a Triangle</h2>
  <p>The semiperimeter looks like a mere half-measure, yet it carries most of the area calculation. Understanding why geometry keeps reaching for half the perimeter prepares the ground for the proof.</p>

  <h3>Definition of Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is exactly half the total perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} />. For a triangle this becomes <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} />. The perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> is the full boundary length, the sum of the three sides; the semiperimeter is that sum halved. The two are related by <span set:html={rich('[[P=2s]]')} />, and confusing them is the single most expensive input mistake on this page.</p>

  <h3>How to Calculate Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> Add the three sides first, then divide once: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6}]]]')} />. The addition must come before the division because the semiperimeter halves the whole boundary, not each side separately. Adding after dividing would still give the same number here, but the formula <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> states the correct order: sum the boundary, then halve it.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Semiperimeter Used Instead of Full Perimeter?</h3>
  <p>Triangle area formulas keep producing halves: each small triangle in the proof contributes a division by two, and the semiperimeter absorbs all of them at once. It is the natural variable of Heron&apos;s formula, of the inradius relation <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}]]')} />, and of the proof that follows. Entering the full perimeter instead would double the result, because <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times P=2rs]]')} /> is twice the true area. The calculator asks for <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />, precisely so the multiplication needs no further adjustment.</p>

  <h3>Can Semiperimeter Be Smaller Than the Longest Side?</h3>
  <p>No. The triangle inequality requires <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: the two shorter sides together must exceed the longest side, or the triangle cannot close. Adding <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to both sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c>2c]]')} />, and halving gives <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{a+b+c}{2}>c]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[s>c]].')} /> The semiperimeter always exceeds the longest side in a valid triangle. If a reported semiperimeter is smaller than the hypotenuse, the inputs cannot describe a real triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Deriving the main area formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is the Area of a Triangle Equal to Inradius Times Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>Start with the general rule <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}}{2}]].')} /> Join the incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> to all three vertices. The triangle splits into three smaller triangles, each using one original side as its base and the inradius as its perpendicular height. The rest of this section computes each piece and adds them, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> is seen, not just memorized.</p>

  <h3>Area of the First Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The triangle built on side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> has base <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, because the radius meets side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> perpendicularly at its tangent point. Its area is therefore <span set:html={rich('[[K_1=\\frac{ar}{2}]].')} /> Nothing about the right angle is used yet; this step works for the side of any triangle.</p>

  <h3>Area of the Second Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The triangle built on side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> follows the same pattern: base <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, perpendicular height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, area <span set:html={rich('[[K_2=\\frac{br}{2}]].')} /> The height is the same number <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> again, because the incenter is equidistant from every side. That shared height is the key to the whole proof.</p>

  <h3>Area of the Third Smaller Triangle</h3>
  <p>The triangle built on side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> completes the set: <span set:html={rich('[[K_3=\\frac{cr}{2}]].')} /> The three smaller triangles tile the original triangle exactly, with no overlap and no gap: every point of the original lies in one of them, since the segments from the incenter to the vertices partition the interior.</p>

  <h3>Adding the Three Areas</h3>
  <p>Add the pieces: <span set:html={rich('[[K=K_1+K_2+K_3]].')} /> Substituting gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ar}{2}+\\frac{br}{2}+\\frac{cr}{2}]].')} /> Factor <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> out of the sum: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{r(a+b+c)}{2}]].')} /> Recall that <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, so the bracketed fraction is exactly the semiperimeter, and <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=r\\times s}]]]')} />. Each halving from the three small triangles has been absorbed into the semiperimeter, which is why the final multiplication needs no further division.</p>

  <h3>Why Does This Formula Work for Every Triangle?</h3>
  <p>Every nondegenerate triangle has an incircle and an incenter, and the incenter&apos;s perpendicular distance to all three sides is the same number <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /> The three-triangle decomposition uses nothing else: no right angle, no particular side lengths, no special shape. Whether the triangle is right, acute, or obtuse, the incenter joins to the vertices and the same algebra lands on <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]].')} /> The formula is a property of triangles in general, which is why geometry textbooks prove it once and use it everywhere.</p>

  <h3>Why Is This Formula Particularly Useful for Right Triangles?</h3>
  <p>The identity stays the same, but the inradius becomes easy. In a general triangle the inradius is usually found from the area itself through <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}]]')} />, which is circular if the area is the goal. A right triangle escapes the circle: its inradius follows directly from the sides via <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />, derived in the next section. That makes <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> a genuine working method for right triangles rather than a rearranged definition.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('proof-area-rs.svg')} alt="Triangle split from incenter I into three smaller triangles of height r, proving K = r times s" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three smaller triangles share the height r; their areas add up to r times s.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Right-triangle inradius -->
<section id="sec-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Inradius of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>For a right triangle with legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />. This shortcut is specific to right triangles. The proof below uses only the tangent-segment property of circles, and the worked examples show it in action.</p>

  <h3>Why Does the Inradius Formula Work?</h3>
  <p>Tangent segments drawn from the same external point to a circle have equal lengths. The incircle touches both legs, and the two radii to those touch points meet the sides at right angles. Together with the right angle at the vertex, the corner region forms a square of side <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, so the distances from the right-angle vertex to the two leg tangency points are each <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /> Call the remaining tangent segment on leg a <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and the remaining one on leg b <span set:html={rich('[[y]].')} /> The two tangent segments from each acute vertex are equal, so the hypotenuse splits into the same <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[y]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[a=r+x]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=r+y]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=x+y]].')} /> Add the first two equations: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=2r+x+y]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[x+y=c]]')} />, this becomes <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=2r+c]]')} />, and rearranging gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example: 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: substitute into the shortcut, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{2}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=1}]]]')} />. The incircle of the 3-4-5 triangle has radius 1, which matches the intro example where <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> gave <span set:html={rich('[[K=6]].')} /></p>

  <h3>Worked Example: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />: then <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{5+12-13}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{4}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=2}]]]')} />. Paired with <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}=15]]')} />, the area follows as <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times15=30]]')} />, confirmed by the legs formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times12}{2}=30]].')} /></p>

  <h3>An Alternative Formula for the Inradius</h3>
  <p>The shortcut can also be written as <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{ab}{a+b+c}}]]]')} />. This is not a second, unrelated formula. Start from <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[rs=\\frac{ab}{2}]].')} /> Since <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />, substitution gives <span set:html={rich('[[r\\frac{a+b+c}{2}=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, and cancelling the halves leaves <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{ab}{a+b+c}]].')} /> It must agree with <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />: check with 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{3\\times4}{3+4+5}=\\frac{12}{12}=1]]')} />, the same radius. Algebraically, <span set:html={rich('[[(a+b-c)(a+b+c)=(a+b)^2-c^2=2ab]]')} /> for a right triangle, so the two forms are identical.</p>

  <h3>Does This Inradius Shortcut Work for Every Triangle?</h3>
  <p>No. The derivation leaned on the right angle: the corner square of side <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> exists only because the two legs meet at 90 degrees. For a general triangle the inradius must come from <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{K}{s}}]]]')} /> or from a formula matched to the known measurements, such as <span set:html={rich('[[r=(s-a)\\tan\\frac{A}{2}]].')} /> Applying <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> to a non-right triangle computes a number with no geometric meaning.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-derivation.svg')} alt="Tangent segments x and y derive the inradius formula r = (a + b - c) / 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Equal tangent segments give a = r + x, b = r + y, c = x + y.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area From Semiperimeter Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator needs exactly two inputs, entered in order. The steps below follow the on-screen layout from identifying the measurements to a checked result.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Determine the inradius of the right triangle.</strong> This is the radius of the circle inscribed in the triangle. If the three sides are known, compute it with <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]].')} /></li>
    <li><strong>Determine its semiperimeter.</strong> Add the three sides and divide by two: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]].')} /> This is half the full perimeter, not the perimeter itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Make sure both values belong to the same triangle.</strong> An inradius from one triangle and a semiperimeter from another produce a meaningless product.</li>
    <li><strong>Convert the measurements into compatible units if necessary.</strong> Both lengths must use the same unit: metres with metres, centimetres with centimetres, never mixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the inradius r into the first input field.</strong> This is the smaller of the two values in any valid triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the semiperimeter s into the second input field.</strong> Double-check that the value is half the perimeter, not the whole perimeter.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong> The calculator multiplies the two inputs directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the displayed result.</strong> Read the area and confirm that it looks reasonable for the triangle size.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the formula and substitution steps.</strong> They show <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> with the entered values filled in.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm that the result is expressed in square units.</strong> Length units multiplied give square units: m², cm², ft², matching the inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset before entering another calculation.</strong> It clears both fields for a clean start.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator has exactly two inputs, inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]].')} /> It does not take the three sides, the legs, the hypotenuse, or an angle. The multiplication <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> is complete as shown; the result must never be divided by two again.</p>
</section>

