<!-- Section 9: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="sec-practical" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Area From Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Twelve worked situations carry the formula from the page into the workshop, the site, and the studio. Each one names a right-triangular panel, section, profile, or simplified model, works the inradius and semiperimeter through the formula, and states the assumption behind the numbers. These are simplified right-triangular models: not every real window, roof, garden, ramp, sail, or land parcel forms an exact right triangle, and an irregular object must be divided into simpler shapes before the formula applies. The area describes the flat geometric surface only; it never establishes structural strength, load capacity, or construction safety on its own.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Triangular Glass Window</h3>
  <p>A triangular glass window has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6\\text{ m}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}=1\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The result represents the flat triangular glass surface before frame allowances are subtracted. Assumption: the pane is a true right triangle; the frame dimensions are handled separately.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Steel Support Bracket</h3>
  <p>A triangular steel support plate has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}=15\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{5+12-13}{2}=2\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. This is the flat plate area and does not establish its structural strength. Assumption: the bracket blank is cut as a right triangle; bolt holes and bends are excluded.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Roof Support Section</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular roof support section has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=15\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{8+15+17}{2}=20\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{8+15-17}{2}=3\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=3\\times20]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=60\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. The formula measures the flat triangular support section, useful for material estimates. Assumption: the section is modeled as a right triangle; the full roof geometry may differ.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Triangular Garden Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular garden section has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=7\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=24\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=25\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=28\\text{ m}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=3\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=3\\times28]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=84\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The area helps estimate the triangular landscaping section, for example when ordering turf or mulch. Assumption: the bed boundaries are straight and meet at a right angle.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Triangular Ramp Profile</h3>
  <p>A simplified ramp side profile has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=9\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=15\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{9+12+15}{2}=18\\text{ m}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{9+12-15}{2}=3\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=3\\times18]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=54\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This measures the triangular side profile, not the sloping ramp surface. Assumption: the profile is modeled with a square corner at the base.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Plywood Cutting Template</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular plywood template has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=16\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=20\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=24\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=4\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=4\\times24]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=96\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. The calculation estimates the flat material area before cutting waste. Assumption: the template is a true right triangle; saw kerf and offcuts are extra.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Triangular Sign Panel</h3>
  <p>A triangular sign panel has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=20\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=21\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=29\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{20+21+29}{2}=35\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{20+21-29}{2}=6\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=6\\times35]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=210\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. This represents the flat printable surface of the panel. Assumption: the panel face is flat and right-triangular; mounting hardware is excluded.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Land Surveying Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular land section has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=9\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=40\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=41\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=45\\text{ m}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=4\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=4\\times45]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=180\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The formula calculates the area of the surveyed triangular section. Assumption: the section is modeled with a square corner; an irregular lot needs subdivision into simpler shapes.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Triangular Fabric Sail</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular fabric panel has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=15\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=20\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=25\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=30\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=5\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=5\\times30]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=150\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. The result excludes seams, hems, and material allowances. Assumption: the sail is modeled flat; real sails curve under load.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Industrial Support Panel</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular industrial panel has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=11\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=60\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=61\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=66\\text{ m}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=5\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=5\\times66]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=330\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The result describes the flat geometric panel rather than its structural performance. Assumption: the panel is modeled as a right triangle; thickness and fixings are separate concerns.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Warehouse Roof-End Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified warehouse roof-end section has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=13\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=84\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=85\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=91\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=6\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=6\\times91]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=546\\text{ ft}^2}]]]')} />. The result estimates the triangular end section, for example when pricing cladding. Assumption: the gable end is modeled as a right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Large Fabrication Panel</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular fabrication panel has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=28\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=45\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=53\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{28+45+53}{2}=63\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{28+45-53}{2}=10\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=10\\times63]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=630\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. This method calculates the panel&apos;s flat triangular area directly from its circle and boundary measurements. Assumption: the panel edges are straight and meet at a right angle.</p>

