<!-- Section 10: Comparing with the legs formula -->
<section id="sec-legs-comparison" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Inradius Formula Give the Same Area as the Legs Formula?</h2>
  <p>Two different formulas, one triangle, one area. Start with <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> and the standard right-triangle area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, which forces <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{rs=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. Both sides measure the same enclosed surface using different geometric information: one reads the incircle and the boundary, the other reads the two perpendicular legs.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Comparison Using a 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Inradius method: <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]].')} /> Legs method: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]].')} /> The answers agree because the inradius and semiperimeter encode the same side information as the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> are built from <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so their product must reconstruct the area the legs describe. Agreement here is not a coincidence; it is algebra.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Inradius Method More Useful?</h3>
  <p>The inradius method wins when r and s are already in hand. Geometry problems about incircles hand over the inradius directly. A proof that needs the area expressed through circle measurements uses <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> naturally. Competition problems often supply the semiperimeter as part of the setup. And as a cross-check, computing the area two independent ways catches arithmetic slips that a single method would hide. In each case the point is input convenience: start from the measurements you have.</p>

  <h3>When Is the Legs Formula Simpler?</h3>
  <p>If both perpendicular legs are already known, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> is usually more direct: one multiplication, one halving, no intermediate r or s to compute. Deriving the inradius and semiperimeter first and then multiplying adds two steps that change nothing. The semiperimeter method is not always the easiest route; it is the best route when the incircle or the boundary is the natural starting point, and a detour when the legs are sitting in front of you.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Heron's formula -->
<section id="sec-heron" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Difference Between Heron&apos;s Formula and Area = Inradius × Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>Heron&apos;s formula computes the area from the three side lengths: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}]]]')} />. It requires the semiperimeter but not the inradius, and it works for any valid triangle. The inradius formula <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} /> needs the inradius and the semiperimeter instead of the three sides. The difference is one of inputs, not of geometry: both return the same enclosed area, because the two formulas are linked through <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}]].')} /> Heron&apos;s formula is the natural choice when all three sides are known and no circle data exists; the inradius formula is natural when the incircle is part of the picture.</p>

  <h3>Worked Comparison</h3>
  <p>Use a 3-4-5 triangle. Heron&apos;s formula: <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. Inradius formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6}]]]')} />. The answers match because Heron&apos;s expression simplifies, through the same three-triangle decomposition, to <span set:html={rich('[[r\\times s]]')} />: the square root of <span set:html={rich('[[s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)]]')} /> equals <span set:html={rich('[[rs]]')} /> whenever r and s come from the same triangle. Do not confuse the two tools, though: Heron&apos;s formula never asks for the inradius, and the inradius shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> belongs to right triangles only.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Input consistency -->
<section id="sec-consistency" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether the Inradius and Semiperimeter Are Valid</h2>
  <p>The calculator accepts two values and multiplies them, but the product is the area of a right triangle only when both values describe the same triangle. The checks below separate valid inputs from impostors.</p>

  <h3>Basic Input Conditions</h3>
  <p>Both inputs must be positive: <span set:html={rich('[[r>0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s>0]].')} /> A length cannot be zero or negative, so a non-positive entry has no geometric meaning. Zero would collapse the triangle to a line with no area; a negative value describes nothing at all. Re-measure or re-derive any input that fails this test.</p>

  <h3>The Semiperimeter Must Be Greater Than the Inradius</h3>
  <p>In a valid triangle the semiperimeter dwarfs the inradius. From <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> with positive legs, and from the geometry of the incircle fitting inside the triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is always clearly larger than <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />: for the 3-4-5 triangle, 6 against 1; for 5-12-13, 15 against 2. The values must also correspond to a valid set of three sides, so this comparison is a quick filter, not the whole validation. An inradius larger than the semiperimeter cannot come from any triangle.</p>

  <h3>Advanced Right-Triangle Consistency Check</h3>
  <p>For a stricter test, a right triangle must satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{r}{s}\\leq3-2\\sqrt{2}}]]]')} />, approximately <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{r}{s}\\leq0.1716}]]]')} />. The idea is simple: from a given r and s, the two legs must be the positive solutions of a quadratic built from <span set:html={rich('[[a+b=s+r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[ab=2rs]]')} />, and that quadratic has real solutions only when its discriminant stays non-negative. The discriminant condition simplifies to exactly this bound. Check 3-4-5: <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{1}{6}\\approx0.1667\\leq0.1716]]')} />, valid. This is an optional refinement for careful work, not a mandatory part of the beginner&apos;s calculation.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When the Inputs Are Inconsistent?</h3>
  <p>Multiplying two positive numbers always produces a numerical result, but that does not prove they represent the inradius and semiperimeter of the same right triangle. An r from one triangle paired with an s from another yields a product with no geometric meaning, however tidy the arithmetic looks. Unless the calculator implementation has been tested for validation behavior, do not assume it checks the relationship: run the checks above yourself, recompute r and s from the sides when the sides are known, and cross-check the area with the legs formula.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Units -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Measurement Units Affect Inradius and Semiperimeter Calculations</h2>
  <p>The inradius and semiperimeter are both lengths, so they follow the same unit rules as any other length measurement. The square unit in the answer comes from multiplying two length units, which makes consistent units the whole game.</p>

  <h3>Why Must Both Inputs Use Compatible Units?</h3>
  <p>The product <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> multiplies a length by a length. If r is in centimetres and s is in metres, the product mixes the two scales and the number has no clean meaning. Convert first so both inputs share one unit: metres, centimetres, millimetres, feet, inches, or yards, but the same one for both. The calculator has unit selectors for this reason: set them before entering the numbers.</p>

  <h3>Worked Unit Conversion Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[r=10\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=1\\text{ m}]].')} /> Convert the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[1\\text{ m}=100\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[K=10\\times100]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=1000\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. Alternatively, convert the inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[10\\text{ cm}=0.1\\text{ m}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[K=0.1\\times1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.1\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. Both answers describe the same area, since <span set:html={rich('[[0.1\\text{ m}^2=1000\\text{ cm}^2]].')} /> Convert first, multiply second, and either unit path lands in the right place.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Result Expressed in Square Units?</h3>
  <p>Area counts unit squares covering the surface: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{cm}\\times\\text{cm}=\\text{cm}^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{m}\\times\\text{m}=\\text{m}^2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{ft}\\times\\text{ft}=\\text{ft}^2]].')} /> The unit is always the length unit squared, because the product of two lengths is a count of squares. Reporting an area as a plain length, such as 1000 cm, is a category error: 1000 cm is a length, while 1000 cm² is a surface.</p>

  <h3>Working With Decimal Values</h3>
  <p>The formula handles decimals without modification. Given <span set:html={rich('[[r=2.5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=8.4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2.5\\times8.4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=21\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Whole numbers, decimals, and fine workshop measurements all flow through the same multiplication. Keep full precision through the calculation and round only the final answer to the accuracy the measurements justify.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Area From Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Nearly every wrong answer in this topic traces to a confused label rather than bad arithmetic. The sixteen mistakes below are the ones that appear again and again, each with its cause and its fix.</p>

  <h3>1. Confusing Semiperimeter With Full Perimeter</h3>
  <p>Entering <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} /> where <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> belongs doubles the answer: <span set:html={rich('[[1\\times12=12]]')} /> instead of 6. The mistake happens because everyday language says perimeter while the formula wants half of it. Fix: always halve the boundary sum before entering it, and check that the entered s is smaller than the perimeter.</p>

  <h3>2. Using Circumradius Instead of Inradius</h3>
  <p>The circumradius of the 3-4-5 triangle is <span set:html={rich('[[R=\\frac{5}{2}=2.5]]')} />; using it gives <span set:html={rich('[[2.5\\times6=15]]')} />, nearly triple the true area. The mistake comes from the two circles sharing the word radius. Fix: confirm the radius belongs to the circle inside the triangle, touching all three sides, not the one through the vertices.</p>

  <h3>3. Forgetting to Calculate the Semiperimeter Correctly</h3>
  <p>Adding only two sides, or dividing each side by two before adding, produces a wrong s. For 3-4-5, adding 3 + 4 and dividing gives 3.5, and <span set:html={rich('[[1\\times3.5=3.5]]')} /> is meaningless. Fix: sum all three sides first, then divide once: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]].')} /></p>

  <h3>4. Dividing the Final Result by Two Again</h3>
  <p>After <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]]')} />, an extra halving gives 3. The habit comes from the legs formula, where the division is explicit. Fix: remember that the halves are already absorbed into the semiperimeter; <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> is complete.</p>

  <h3>5. Using the Wrong Right-Triangle Inradius Formula</h3>
  <p>Guessing <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{c-a-b}{2}]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b}{2}]]')} /> produces a wrong radius. For 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{5-3-4}{2}=-1]]')} /> is not even a length. Fix: use <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> with a and b as the legs and c as the hypotenuse, exactly in that arrangement.</p>

  <h3>6. Applying the Right-Triangle Inradius Shortcut to an Ordinary Triangle</h3>
  <p>The shortcut leans on the right angle, so on a general triangle it computes a number with no geometric meaning. Fix: for non-right triangles find the inradius from <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}]]')} /> or a formula matched to the known measurements, never from <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]].')} /></p>

  <h3>7. Entering the Inradius and Semiperimeter From Different Triangles</h3>
  <p>Taking r = 1 from a 3-4-5 triangle and s = 15 from a 5-12-13 triangle gives 15, a product that describes no real triangle. The mistake happens when values are copied from different worked examples or different drawings. Fix: derive or measure both inputs from the same triangle before multiplying.</p>

  <h3>8. Mixing Centimetres and Metres</h3>
  <p>Multiplying r = 10 cm by s = 1 m without converting gives 10 of nothing clean. Fix: convert to one unit first: <span set:html={rich('[[1\\text{ m}=100\\text{ cm}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[K=10\\times100=1000\\text{ cm}^2]].')} /></p>

  <h3>9. Reporting the Result in Linear Units</h3>
  <p>An area of 6 m² is not 6 m. The mistake usually comes from copying the input unit without squaring it. Fix: write the squared unit with the number every time: m², cm², ft².</p>

  <h3>10. Entering Zero or Negative Measurements</h3>
  <p>Lengths are positive. Zero collapses the triangle to a line with no area, and negative lengths have no geometric meaning here. Fix: re-measure or re-derive the input; reject non-positive values before calculating.</p>

  <h3>11. Confusing the Incenter With the Circumcenter</h3>
  <p>The incenter is the meeting point of the angle bisectors; the circumcenter is the meeting point of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides. For a right triangle the circumcenter sits at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, a completely different point. Mixing them up leads to measuring the wrong radius. Fix: the incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> is inside the triangle at equal distance from the sides, not the vertices.</p>

  <h3>12. Assuming the Incircle Touches Only Two Sides</h3>
  <p>A circle wedged into a corner touches two sides, but that is not the incircle. The incircle touches all three, and the area proof needs all three tangent points. Fix: verify three contact points, one per side, before treating a radius as the inradius.</p>

  <h3>13. Forgetting That the Radius Meets Each Tangent Side Perpendicularly</h3>
  <p>The shared-height argument in the proof depends on <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r\\perp c]].')} /> A slanted segment from the incenter to a side is longer than r and cannot serve as the height. Fix: measure the inradius along the perpendicular to the side, never along a diagonal.</p>

  <h3>14. Confusing the Inradius With the Diameter</h3>
  <p>Entering the full width of the incircle as r doubles the radius and doubles the area. For the 3-4-5 triangle, entering 2 instead of 1 gives <span set:html={rich('[[2\\times6=12]].')} /> Fix: the inradius runs from the center to one side, half the circle&apos;s full width.</p>

  <h3>15. Using Heron&apos;s Formula Incorrectly</h3>
  <p>Common slips include forgetting the square root, writing <span set:html={rich('[[s(s+a)(s+b)(s+c)]]')} /> with plus signs, or feeding the full perimeter into the formula. For 3-4-5, omitting the root gives 36 instead of 6. Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}]]')} />, with the semiperimeter, minus signs, and the root all in place.</p>

  <h3>16. Assuming a Numerical Result Automatically Proves the Inputs Are Geometrically Consistent</h3>
  <p>Any two positive numbers multiply, so the calculator always returns something. That something is the area of a right triangle only when r and s truly belong to one. Fix: run the consistency checks, recompute r and s from the sides when possible, and cross-check with the legs formula before trusting the number.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]].')} /> Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]].')} /> Incorrectly dividing by two again: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{1\\times6}{2}=3]]')} />, wrong because the halving is already inside the semiperimeter. Incorrectly entering the full perimeter 12 instead of the semiperimeter 6: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times12=12]]')} />, double the true area. The arithmetic looks confident in all three cases; only the geometry tells them apart.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Right Triangle Area Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Six independent checks, all demonstrated on the 3-4-5 triangle (<span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=6]]')} />). Agreement between different routes is the strongest evidence an answer is right, in homework and in practical measurement alike.</p>

  <h3>Method 1: Check the Main Formula</h3>
  <p>Run <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> fresh: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]].')} /> A second pass with the numbers re-entered catches typos and mis-keyed digits.</p>

  <h3>Method 2: Calculate the Semiperimeter From Three Sides</h3>
  <p>Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> from the sides: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]].')} /> If the recomputed s differs from the entered one, the input was wrong and the product must be redone.</p>

  <h3>Method 3: Calculate the Inradius From the Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}=1]].')} /> A mismatch against the entered r means the radius came from a different triangle or a misapplied formula.</p>

  <h3>Method 4: Verify Using the Standard Legs Formula</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]].')} /> This route uses completely different inputs, so agreement with <span set:html={rich('[[rs]]')} /> confirms both the inputs and the arithmetic.</p>

  <h3>Method 5: Verify Using Heron&apos;s Formula</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}=6]].')} /> Heron&apos;s formula exercises the sides and the semiperimeter together, catching side-label errors the other checks might miss.</p>

  <h3>Method 6: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25=5^2]].')} /> This checks the side identification behind every other calculation; a wrong hypotenuse means a side was mislabeled and r, s, and K all need rebuilding.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: Applications in practice -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of the Inradius and Semiperimeter Formula</h2>
  <p>The same two-input pattern shows up wherever a triangular panel or section needs measuring and the incircle data is the convenient starting point. A glazier sizes a window from its edge lengths; a fabricator reads the legs of a bracket blank; a surveyor reduces a lot corner to three straight sides. From those sides come the inradius and the semiperimeter, and one multiplication names the area. The four panels below show the pattern at different scales: a glass window (6 m²), a roof support (60 ft²), a garden section (84 m²), and a steel plate (30 cm²), each labelled with its inradius, its semiperimeter, and the resulting area.</p>

