<!-- Conclusion -->
<section id="sec-conclusion" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Two Measurements, One Product</h2>
  <p>An inradius and a semiperimeter are enough. Multiply them, and the enclosed surface is named. The three-triangle proof shows why no division follows the multiplication, the tangent-segment argument shows why the right triangle gets its inradius shortcut, and the legs formula plus Heron&apos;s formula stand ready to check every answer.</p>
  <p>The working habit is simple: confirm both inputs describe the same triangle, match the units, keep decimals unrounded until the end, and verify through a second method. With those habits, <span set:html={rich('[[K=r\\times s]]')} /> is one of the most dependable area calculations in geometry.</p>
</section>

